## Purpose
- Give fast operational commands for local bring-up and smoke checks.

## Source of truth
- `aspire/TeamHub.AppHost/Program.cs`
- `docker-compose.yml`
- `docker-compose.dev.yml`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/.env.example`
- `services/team-hub-gateway/team-hub-gateway/.env.example`
- `frontend/team-hub-web/package.json`
- `services/team-hub-auth/team-hub-auth/Properties/launchSettings.json`
- `services/team-hub-gateway/team-hub-gateway/Properties/launchSettings.json`
- `infrastructure/monitoring/docker-compose.monitoring.yml`
- `infrastructure/monitoring/otel-collector-config.yaml`
- `infrastructure/monitoring/tempo.yaml`
- `infrastructure/monitoring/prometheus.yml`
- `infrastructure/monitoring/.env.example`

## Do
- **Preferred local dev (full stack, one command):**
  - prerequisites: .NET 10 SDK, Aspire 13 (`Aspire.AppHost.Sdk` via NuGet), Docker, Node.js/npm
  - `cd aspire/TeamHub.AppHost && dotnet run`
  - opens Aspire Dashboard (URL in console); endpoints: frontend `https://localhost:4200`, nginx API `https://localhost:8080`, gateway `http://localhost:5000`
  - JWT dev config: `aspire/TeamHub.AppHost/appsettings.Development.json` (`Aspire:Jwt:*`)
  - stop with `Ctrl+C` in AppHost terminal
- Local dev database and Redis (manual workflow, separate from prod-like data):
  - `docker compose -f docker-compose.dev.yml up -d`
  - compose project `team-hub-dev` (isolated from compose project `team-hub`)
  - containers `db-postgres-dev` (postgres on `localhost:5433`, databases `auth_db` + `organization_db`) and `cache-redis-dev` (`localhost:6379`)
  - then `dotnet run` / `npm start` with Development config (auth auto-migrates on start)
- Production-like full stack via docker compose project `team-hub` (ready build before deployment):
  - `./scripts/setup-certs.sh` (mkcert = trusted HTTPS in browser; otherwise self-signed with warning)
  - copy each `.env.example` to `.env` in auth and gateway service directories
  - set `Jwt__Key` to at least 32 characters (same values in auth and gateway)
  - `docker compose up --build` (project `team-hub`)
- Docker runs with `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production` and `appsettings.Production.json`.
- TLS certs are shared from `./certs` into `ui-web` and `gw-nginx` containers.
- Use `https://localhost:4200`, `https://localhost:8080` (API edge), and `http://localhost:5000` (gateway debug).
- Each microservice keeps its own `.env` (not in repo root).
- Expected host ports after start:
  - frontend `https://localhost:4200` (`ui-web-prod`)
  - infrastructure nginx `https://localhost:8080` (`gw-nginx-prod`, API entry)
  - gateway `http://localhost:5000` (`gw-api-prod`, debug only)
  - auth (direct) `http://localhost:5001` (`srv-auth-prod`); health `GET http://localhost:5001/health`
  - redis `localhost:6379` (`cache-redis-dev` / `cache-redis-prod`)
  - postgres `localhost:5433` (`db-postgres-prod`, databases `authdb` + `organizationdb`)
  - monitoring Grafana `http://localhost:3000` (`mon-grafana-prod` / `mon-grafana-dev`)
  - monitoring Loki `http://localhost:3100` (`mon-loki-prod` / `mon-loki-dev`)
  - monitoring Tempo `http://localhost:3200` (`mon-tempo-prod` / `mon-tempo-dev`)
  - monitoring Prometheus `http://localhost:9090` (`mon-prometheus-prod` / `mon-prometheus-dev`)
  - monitoring OTel Collector OTLP gRPC `localhost:4317`, HTTP `localhost:4318`
- Frontend local dev (from `frontend/team-hub-web`):
  - `npm install`
  - start infrastructure nginx + gateway + auth + postgres (e.g. `docker compose up gw-nginx gw-api srv-auth db-postgres`)
  - `npm start` (uses proxy to infrastructure nginx on `https://localhost:8080`)
- Optional standalone Redis (without root compose):
  - `cp infrastructure/redis/.env.example infrastructure/redis/.env`
  - `docker compose -f infrastructure/redis/docker-compose.redis.yml --env-file infrastructure/redis/.env up -d`
- Optional standalone monitoring (without root compose):
  - `cp infrastructure/monitoring/.env.example infrastructure/monitoring/.env`
  - `docker compose -f infrastructure/monitoring/docker-compose.monitoring.yml --env-file infrastructure/monitoring/.env up -d`
  - Grafana: `http://localhost:3000` (default user `admin`, password from env file)
  - Loki ready check: `GET http://localhost:3100/ready`
- First telemetry in Grafana (OTLP via collector):
  - start stack: `docker compose up -d mon-otel mon-loki mon-tempo mon-prometheus mon-grafana srv-auth gw-api`
  - generate traffic: `curl http://localhost:5000/health` (gateway) or `curl http://localhost:5001/health` (auth)
  - metrics scrape check: `curl http://localhost:5001/metrics | head`
  - open Grafana `http://localhost:3000` (credentials from `infrastructure/monitoring/compose.prod.env`)
  - Explore -> **Loki** -> `{service_name="team-hub-auth"}` or `{service_name="team-hub-gateway"}`
  - Explore -> **Tempo** -> search by TraceId from `X-Correlation-ID` response header
  - Explore -> **Prometheus** -> `http_server_request_duration_seconds_count`
  - note: prod logs are pushed via OTLP (not Docker stdout scraping); `dotnet run` logs stay local unless OTLP endpoint is reachable
- Quick auth smoke through infrastructure nginx:
  - `POST https://localhost:8080/api/auth/register`
  - `POST https://localhost:8080/api/auth/login`
  - `POST https://localhost:8080/api/auth/refresh` (with cookie)
  - `POST https://localhost:8080/api/auth/logout`
- Quick auth smoke through gateway (debug):
  - `POST http://localhost:5000/api/auth/register`
  - `POST http://localhost:5000/api/auth/login`
  - `POST http://localhost:5000/api/auth/refresh` (with cookie)
  - `POST http://localhost:5000/api/auth/logout`
- Quick auth smoke through frontend nginx proxy:
  - `POST https://localhost:4200/api/auth/register`
- Debug prod request by Correlation ID:
  - reproduce in UI and copy `X-Correlation-ID` from browser DevTools (Network tab, request or response headers)
  - or call API directly and read response header: `curl -s -D - -o /dev/null -H "X-Correlation-ID: test-id" http://localhost:5000/health`
  - open Grafana `http://localhost:3000` -> Explore -> Loki
  - query both services: `{service_name=~"team-hub-gateway|team-hub-auth"} | json | CorrelationId="<trace-id>"`
  - gateway proxy lines include `CorrelationId` in structured log; auth request logs include the same field
  - if log lines look like `[09:14:47 INF] team_hub_auth...` (plain text), the container is not using Production Serilog JSON — rebuild with `docker compose up --build srv-auth gw-api` and confirm `ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production`
  - in Grafana, use `| json` to extract `CorrelationId`; plain-text log lines do not show structured fields in the message body
  - after deploy, a prod log line should start with `{"@t":` (compact JSON); search `{service="auth", env="prod"} |= "\"CorrelationId\""` to confirm the field exists
  - register flow uses one Correlation ID for both `/register` and auto-login `/login`; query `{service=~"gateway|auth", env="prod"} | json | CorrelationId="<uuid>" | RequestPath=~"/api/auth/(register|login)"`
  - filter auth logs by user: `{service="auth", env="prod"} | json | UserId="019f5fe8-2b37-749d-8680-009f8cc5e67f"` (requires Bearer JWT on request; cookie-only login/register have empty `UserId`)
  - filter auth logs by visit: `{service="auth", env="prod"} | json | SessionId="<session-id>"` (copy `X-Session-ID` from DevTools; same value for all requests in one tab visit)
  - debug unhandled errors: copy `correlationId` from API error response (`application/problem+json`) and query `{service="auth", env="prod"} | json | CorrelationId="<id>" | @l="Error"`
- Health checks (reduce Grafana/console noise):
  - auth Docker healthcheck interval is `120s` (`docker-compose.yml` + auth `Dockerfile`)
  - `/health` and `/metrics` requests are excluded from Serilog request logging in gateway and auth
  - gateway logs `/health` only when unhealthy
  - after `docker compose up --build -d srv-auth gw-api`, Loki should not show `/health` spam every few seconds

## Don't
- Do not keep secrets in repo root `.env` or `.env.production`.
- Do not use full `docker compose` for daily local dev when Aspire AppHost is available; use AppHost or `docker-compose.dev.yml` for DB plus `dotnet run` / `npm start`.
- Do not start compose project `team-hub-dev` and compose project `team-hub` at the same time (both bind postgres to `5433`).
- Do not run heavy builds/tests for docs-only updates.
- Do not verify auth flow by bypassing gateway when checking end-to-end.
- Do not run AppHost and `docker compose` on the same host ports at once.

## Checklist
- Containers healthy (`db-postgres-prod`, `srv-auth-prod`, `srv-organization-prod`, `gw-api-prod`, `gw-nginx-prod`, `ui-web-prod`).
- Gateway route `/api/auth/*` reaches auth service.
- Frontend can authenticate with cookie-based refresh flow.
